.TH MU2EDAQ-CHECKS.YAML 5 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
checks.yaml \- health-check thresholds, expectations and profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I config/checks.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
Says what "healthy" means: the thresholds a check compares against, the
filesystems and services a node is expected to have, and which checks apply to
which class of host.
.PP
The semantics were taken from
.I mu2edaq-operations/scripts/check_functions.sh
so that a node this tool calls healthy is also healthy by the on-node script's
standard.
.PP
A check id listed here with no implementation is reported as
.B skip
with an explanation, never silently dropped: an unimplemented check must not
look like a passing one.
.BR "mu2e-power-recovery \-\-list\-checks"
prints what is actually implemented.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS thresholds
.TP
.IR disk_full_pct ", " disk_warn_pct
Filesystem usage that fails and warns. Defaults 90 and 80; 90 matches the
on-node script.
.TP
.I data_link_speed_mbps
Expected data-network link rate, from
.IR /sys/class/net/<nic>/speed .
Default 10000. A link that renegotiated at 1 Gb/s after a switch reboot is a
real and quiet failure: everything works, ten times too slowly.
.TP
.I load_per_core_warn
Load average per core above which a freshly booted node is suspicious.
.TP
.I max_post_boot_uptime_s
Uptime above which a node that phase 2 just powered on is flagged \(em it did
not actually reboot, so the IPMI command probably reached a different chassis.
.TP
.IR ping_count ", " ping_timeout_s ", " ssh_connect_timeout_s ", " ipmi_timeout_s
Per-probe budgets.
.SS mounts
.TP
.I common
Filesystems every node should have.
.TP
.IR mc2 ", " mc1 ", " teststand
Additional per-location expectations.
.TP
.I paths
Plain existence checks, which catch a half-mounted tree that
.BR mountpoint (1)
alone would pass.
.PP
A mount entry may name a
.IR server .
The check is then skipped on that host \(em
.I mu2e-mgr-01
does not NFS-mount its own
.I /home
\(em and, in the
.I disk.nfs_from_mgr
check, the actual mount source is compared against it. That is not the same
test as
.IR disk.mounts :
a stale entry can leave
.I /home
mounted from an address that no longer serves it, which passes
.B mountpoint
and fails the moment anything reads a file.
.SS services
Daemons whose absence means the node is up but not usable, checked by
.IR svc.running .
.I common
applies everywhere; the other keys are
.I host classes
from
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5),
not profile names, and are added to
.IR common .
A host class with no entry and no
.I common
list makes
.I svc.running
report
.B skip
rather than pass.
.PP
.I systemctl is-active
is tried first and
.BR pgrep (1)
used as the fallback, because some of these run from puppet-managed init
scripts rather than as systemd units and would otherwise be reported missing
while running.
.SS profiles
Check ids per profile.
.I base
is merged into every other profile and comes first, because its members \(em
reachability and login \(em are the ones whose failure explains every later
failure. A profile lists only what it adds on top of
.IR base .
.PP
The shipped profiles are
.BR base ", " gateway ", " manager ", " datalogger ", " dcs ", " cfo ,
.BR readout ", " teststand " and " other .
.PP
Which one a node gets depends on the phase:
.IP \(bu 3
phase 1 uses the profile named after the node's host class, or
.B other
when no profile has that name. The detector classes
.RB ( tracker ", " calorimeter ", " crv ", " stm ", " extmon ", " worker )
have no profile of their own, so a bare survey gives them
.BR base " + " other ;
.IP \(bu 3
phase 2 uses the stage's
.I checks
key from
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5),
which is how those nodes get
.B readout
at all.
.PP
Phase 3 runs no profile: it is the mesh probe below and nothing else.
.PP
Adding a class to
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5)
without adding a profile of the same name here is therefore silently a no-op
for phase 1.
.SS mesh
Phase 3 settings.
.TP
.I networks
Each entry gives a network
.IR name ,
whether to probe a
.I full_mesh
or sample against anchors, and whether to run an
.IR mtu_probe .
The data network is full-mesh with an MTU probe; the lab and IPMI networks are
sampled, which is O(N) rather than O(N^2).
.TP
.I anchors
Representative targets used when
.I full_mesh
is false. An anchor that is not in this run's node set \(em because
.B \-\-node
or
.B \-\-location
narrowed it \(em would leave the probe aiming at nothing, so when none of them
is present the first three nodes on the network are used instead. The network
still gets tested; the report names what was actually probed.
.TP
.I mtu_payload_bytes
ICMP payload for the jumbo-frame probe. 8972 plus an 8-byte ICMP header plus a
20-byte IP header is exactly a 9000-byte frame, sent with DF set, so it fails
if any hop on the path is not jumbo-clean.
.TP
.IR count ", " timeout_s
Per-pair probe budget: ICMP echoes per pair, and the per-packet wait in
seconds. Unset, they fall back to
.IR thresholds.ping_count " and " thresholds.ping_timeout_s .
They also size the wall-clock bound on the single SSH session that carries a
source's whole probe list, so raising either lengthens phase 3 by roughly
.RI ( targets " x " count " x " timeout_s )
per source.
.SH THE IMPLEMENTED CHECKS
The 25 ids a profile may name. The ten marked
.RB ( root )
run their commands over the root session
.RI ( ssh.root_user ,
authenticated from
.IR kerberos.root_principal ).
Where that login is refused they fail one at a time, so putting many of them
in a profile for hosts you have no root on produces a column of failures, not
one.
.TP
.B disk.errors (root)
No storage errors in the kernel log since boot.
.TP
.B disk.local
No local filesystem is at or above
.IR disk_full_pct .
.TP
.B disk.mounts
Every filesystem in
.I mounts
is mounted, and every path in
.I mounts.paths
exists.
.TP
.B disk.nfs_from_mgr
The NFS mounts really come from the host named in the mount entry's
.IR server ,
not merely from something. A stale entry passes
.I disk.mounts
and fails the moment anything reads a file.
.TP
.B disk.raid (root)
md/RAID arrays are complete and not rebuilding.
.TP
.B disk.smart (root)
SMART overall health for every physical device.
.TP
.B host.kernel
Kernel release, and no taint flags.
.TP
.B host.uptime
Uptime and load average, including the post-power-on test against
.IR max_post_boot_uptime_s .
.TP
.B login.users (root)
Every account in
.I kerberos.verify_users
can start a session. Run as
.B su - <user>
from root, so it exercises the home directory too: after an outage the usual
failure is not a broken account but an unmounted
.IR /home .
.TP
.B net.data
The data-network interface is up, addressed inside the data subnet, and
negotiated at
.IR data_link_speed_mbps .
.TP
.B net.dns
Forward and reverse name resolution work.
.TP
.B net.forwarding (root)
The gateway is forwarding, with a route for each of its location's subnets.
.TP
.B net.interfaces
Every network the node is on, and for which a CIDR is recorded, has an
interface up with an address in it.
.TP
.B net.ipmi_reach
The gateway has an interface on the IPMI subnet.
.TP
.B pcie.devices (root)
The Xilinx readout card is enumerated on the PCIe bus. A card missing after a
power event is usually a cold-boot training failure, which only a full AC cycle
recovers.
.TP
.B pcie.driver (root)
The mu2e driver is loaded and its device nodes are present.
.TP
.B ping.lab
ICMP reachability on the lab network, probed from the gateway.
.TP
.B power.sel
No new critical entries in the BMC event log since the baseline taken in
phase 1.
.TP
.B power.sensors
No IPMI sensor is in a critical state.
.TP
.B power.status
IPMI chassis power state.
.TP
.B ssh.login
An ordinary SSH session can be opened. Its failure gates the rest: the
remaining ssh checks on that node are recorded
.B unknown
rather than retried, and the detail lists every credential that was refused
with its reason.
.IP
A changed host key is reported as itself, not as a login failure, because no
credential can get past it: after an outage it usually means the node was
reimaged, and the other reading is interception. The result says to verify the
new fingerprint out of band \(em
.B ssh-keyscan <node>
from a gateway \(em before touching
.IR ~/.ssh/known_hosts .
.TP
.B ssh.login_root (root)
A root SSH session can be opened.
.TP
.B svc.firewall (root)
The packet filter has rules loaded.
.B nft
first,
.B iptables
as the fallback. An empty ruleset is a failure rather than "no firewall
configured": a gateway that boots with no rules looks healthy and is wide
open.
.TP
.B svc.nfs_export (root)
The NFS exports are published.
.TP
.B svc.running
Every daemon selected by
.I services
is running.
.PP
.BR "mu2e-power-recovery \-\-list\-checks"
prints this list from the registry itself, which is the copy that cannot go
stale.
.SH EXAMPLES
Warn earlier about disk usage:
.RS
.EX
thresholds:
  disk_warn_pct: 70
.EE
.RE
.PP
Require a new daemon on the data loggers:
.RS
.EX
services:
  datalogger:
    \- node_exporter
    \- mu2e_fts
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the implemented checks and what each verifies:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-list\-checks
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2e-power-netcheck (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
